<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Colony of New Brunswick</h1><p class="st-meta">1784 CE – 1867 CE · capital Fredericton</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A British colony created for Loyalist refugees in 1784, whose timber and shipbuilding made it a founding province of Canada.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a> about 14,000 Loyalists settled along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-john-river">Saint John River</a> in what was then part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-nova-scotia">Colony of Nova Scotia</a>. Wanting a government of their own, they won partition in 1784, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-carleton">Thomas Carleton</a> became the first governor. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fredericton">Fredericton</a>, inland and safer from attack, was chosen as capital over the port of Saint John.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The colony had an elected assembly from 1786 and won responsible government in 1848. Its economy depended on cutting pine for masts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> exported to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>, and on building wooden ships. Irish immigration in the 1840s, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadians">Acadians</a> returning to the north and east, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliseet">Wolastoqiyik</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikmaq">Mi'kmaq</a> communities shaped a mixed society. A boundary dispute with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-maine">Maine</a> was settled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/webster-ashburton-treaty">Webster-Ashburton Treaty</a> of 1842.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Railway hopes and fear of American invasion after the Civil War persuaded New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> to join Confederation. It became one of the four original provinces of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-canada">Canada</a> on 1 July 1867.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/colony-of-new-brunswick.jpg" alt="Colony of New Brunswick" width="320" height="201"><figcaption>Image: Dbenbenn (original) E Pluribus Anthony (current) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_New_Brunswick.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1784 CE – 1867 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Fredericton</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Thomas Carleton</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Protestantism, Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>New Brunswick</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1826&amp;focus=colony-of-new-brunswick" aria-label="Colony of New Brunswick on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3127 539 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Colony of New Brunswick lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3447 795L3483 855M3447 795L4997 651M3447 795L3203 831"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3483 855h0M4997 651h0M3203 831h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3447 795h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3447 795h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1826&amp;focus=colony-of-new-brunswick">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
